Track 2: What You Call a Boundary Line, We Call a Wiretap
- Story & Context: Written during a high-stakes standoff with transit officers along the abandoned rail corridors of the old Industrial Canal, this track serves as the group's philosophical manifesto on borders and state control. Turntablist Maya "Vinyl-Grave" Vance pitted a scratched 1964 Albert Ayler record against a brutal, 90-BPM hardcore drum break while Reductus delivered a whispered, paranoid confession regarding missing city blueprints. The track's pulse relies entirely on a hollow, ringing LP cowbell struck with an iron rod, vibrating through the cold steel beams of the overpass. Reductus leans into the static, asking the listener, "How you gonna draw a border on a wave that’s already inside your chest?"
